        "pkcontributionId": 68307,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are no data available.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-10-27T10:08:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68308,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-10-27T10:08:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68309,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "<p>The pathogenesis is unknown. Unlike in mucoepidermoid carcinoma, <em>MAML2</em> rearrangement has not been detected {24134933}. <em>KIT</em> mutation is only occasionally seen {18486988}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-22T17:00:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68310,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not reported</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-10-27T10:08:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 68311,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 246,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "<p>Adenosquamous carcinoma is a high-grade carcinoma with areas closely resembling non-keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) and areas of adenocarcinoma with definite glandular formations and often containing mucins {10355651; 1689123}. The presence of mucin can be highlighted by mucicarmine staining {24134933}. A minor glandular component (&lt;&nbsp;10%) may be observed in otherwise typical SCC, and such cases should be diagnosed as SCC. Definitive diagnosis requires a resection specimen, but the diagnosis may be suggested based on findings in small biopsies, cytology, or excisional biopsies.</p>\r\n<p>One case of adenosquamous cell carcinoma with a rhabdomyomatous component {15316327} and one case with a sarcomatous component {23158163} have also been reported.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>The tumour is positive for epithelial markers such as cytokeratins and EMA {15316327; 1689123}, and it can be variably positive for CD5 and KIT (CD117) {24134933; 18486988}.&nbsp;The squamous component should be positive for p63/40 and/or CK5/6.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Thymic SCC by definition lacks a glandular component in &ge;&nbsp;10% of the tumour area. Poorly differential mucoepidermoid carcinoma lacks frank squamous differentiation (keratinization, intercellular bridges) and may show <em>MAML2</em> translocations {24134933}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-22T17:00:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
